ren but protesters refused to give  up  they returned to the streets in more  than a dozen cities demanding the return  of their democratically elected leader  aung san tsuchi in the capital the armed  forces held its annual holiday with a  parade  representatives from eight countries  attended including russia  and china the british government is  trying to push ahead with a contentious  bill that would give police new powers  to stop protests  ten people were arrested in the third  night of demonstrations  outside the police station in bristol it  began as a peaceful sit-down  protest but scuffles broke out when  officers tried to break up the crowd  critics of the proposed legislation  argue it infringes on the right to free  and peaceful expression  that has led to a protest movement known  as kill the bill  now to the saga in the suez canal the  ever given ship  is still stuck and still blocking a  vital shipping route  strong tides and winds continue to  complicate efforts to dislodge the ship  from thick  sediment the head of the canal authority  says it's looking to remove  some of over 18 thousand containers on  board to lighten its load  but only as a last resort the ship that  carries cargo between  asia and europe has been stuck in the  canal for five days now  still ahead how young people are giving  the power of poetry  a modern makeover  you're watching global national  hello right now this is the hottest  tourist attraction in iceland  hiking trails have been created for the  thousands of sightseers wanting to get a  glimpse of the fiery lava fountains  the volcano erupted a week ago for the  first time in 800 years  and experts say it could be like this  for years  poetry has exploded in popularity long  viewed as  stuffy and elitist the genre has  inspired a new era of young poets from  diverse backgrounds  and they don't shy from their personal  feelings i spoke with some young poets  across the country  about how the medium is getting a new  makeover  i was in ocean once bare shoulders and  whale song  in my rib cage i watched as the rigs  drilled holes in my body  nisha patel gets paid to write poems  they left no part of my trenches  unmanned rarely is this a career but  patel is edmonton's  poet laureate your job as a poet  laureate is to reflect the times  and the thoughts and the feelings of the  people does the city put any pressure on  you to write about them or  or write put them in a good light the  city has never once put pressure on me  to write about them in a positive way  and i'm happy to say that to this day i  probably haven't done that anyway  here's the proof in this recent attack  on city hall  the city wishes harder shoves body into  a convention center  and hopes the people with the votes are  appeased that no tax dollars have been  wasted  there's a new generation of young  diverse poets in canada  with a lot to say when i didn't have a  bible  my religion teacher called me devil's  daughter  i think there's just a hunger for poetry  because everybody  if you think about it like way back in  the day  poets were actually revered they were  respected  their poems are political personal and  go beyond the pages of a book check out  the full story tonight on the new  reality  at 7 pm feeding the front lines up next  a canadian entrepreneur thanks the  pandemics  unsung heroes  an ontario entrepreneur has found a way  to thank the healthcare workers  at long-term care homes since january  he's made sure hot meals get delivered  to the front lines  every single day and as karen lieberman  reports  this isn't the first time he's been  compelled to help  in the kitchen of this middle eastern  restaurant the team is hard at work  preparing  hot meals it's a meal of gratitude we  call it mohammed fakee is the founder  and ceo of canadian restaurant chain  paramount fine foods in toronto he's  known as much for his business sense  as he is for his social activism and  philanthropy  when i get impacted by something and i  feel it i react by doing something  fakie's focus right now long-term care  a sector hit hard by the covid 19  pandemic  3 700 seniors died in our community and  it was hurting me  you know i grew up in a community where  treating our seniors  says a lot about us fakie's story began  a world away  in beirut lebanon i have been in a war  i've lived in bunkers  and i always came out with a smile  because i believe there is a light that  will come  he came to canada with little more than  a dream  to be successful fakie succeeded and has  dedicated himself  to others from providing hotels for  those experiencing homelessness  to hiring syrian refugees thank you  thank you so much for all your hard work  and now feeding frontline long-term care  staff  to date 11 000 meals have been served  with multiple locations receiving every  day  the goal is 30 thousand meals but that  would depend on donations  [Music]  we don't get to see the appreciation  right firsthand so it's nice when  someone comes and and brings us all  lunch  for kathy mclennan this gesture of  goodwill  speaks volumes i mean it may be a little  thing  but it means so much to us because the  food for the ltc  frontline campaign is less about lunch  more about showing appreciation and  expressing gratitude  to everyone out there giving back feels  good feels amazing  and it does not maybe you i'm not  changing their life  but i'm sure we are making one day  better for them karen lieberman  global news toronto  and that is global national for this  saturday night i'm robin gill  tonight your canada is alice siding in  creston bc  we'd love to see your corner of the  country so please keep sending your  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

